msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: the PHP version with the fixes, such as 8.3.36.
#: includes/Security/Findings.php:126
#, php-format
msgid "PHP, which runs every page of your site, has released fixes for security holes since this version. The fixes are in PHP %s and later, and attackers look for servers that haven’t installed them."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: the PHP version with the fixes, such as 8.3.36.
#: includes/Security/Findings.php:128
#, php-format
msgid "In your hosting panel, choose the newest PHP version it offers (at least %s), or ask your host to update PHP. Some hosts build PHP with the fixes added under an older number: if yours does, ask them to confirm, then mark this as expected. Check the site afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:131
msgid "PHP no longer gets security fixes"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:132
msgid "Each PHP version gets security fixes for a few years. This one no longer does, so holes found in it from now on are never fixed, and newer plugins stop supporting it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:133
msgid "Move to PHP 8.3 or newer in your hosting panel, or ask your host. Update your plugins and theme first, take a backup, and check the site afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:137
msgid "The deep scan found a pattern from hacked websites in this file. A few premium plugins and themes hide their code in similar ways, so check where the file comes from."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:138
msgid "If the file isn’t part of a plugin or theme you installed on purpose, restore that plugin or theme from a fresh copy, delete files you don’t recognize and change your passwords. If it’s from a product you trust, mark it as expected."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:142
msgid "This file is different from the copy the scan recorded. Themes, premium plugins and must-use plugins can’t be checked against WordPress.org, so a change nobody made on purpose is how injected code shows up: one added line can load a script on every page."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:143
msgid "Ask whoever looks after the site whether they changed it. If nobody did, remove the added lines (or restore the file from a backup or a fresh copy of the theme or plugin), turn off the file editor, and change the passwords of every administrator. If the change is expected, mark it as expected: the file as it is now becomes the recorded copy."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:146
msgid "A new file since the last scan"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:147
msgid "This file wasn’t there when the scan recorded this theme or plugin, and it wasn’t added by an update."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:148
msgid "If nobody on your team added it, save a copy, delete it, and change the passwords of every administrator. If it’s expected, mark it as expected."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:150
msgid "A WordPress file was changed"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:150
msgid "WordPress files are the same on every site. A changed one usually means a hack, or a developer edited WordPress itself (which updates undo)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:150
msgid "Dashboard → Updates → Re-install version puts back WordPress’s own files without touching your content. Then run the scan again."
